## Why This Is Asked

This question probes real-world streaming semantics, multi-tenant isolation, and data contracts in GCP with practical rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Dataflow exactly-once processing
- Tenant-level dedupe by (tenant_id, txn_id)
- Watermarks for late events
- Parquet in Cloud Storage + incremental BigQuery loading
- Data Catalog contracts and lineage
- 24h rollback and backfill test plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo Dataflow-like dedupe (illustrative)
class DedupFn {
  process(element) {
    const key = element.tenant_id + '|' + element.txn_id;
    if (!state.has(key)) {
      state.add(key);
      return element;
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify exactly-once behavior under backpressure and late data?
- How would you roll back a fraudulent day’s data within 24h?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes multi-tenant data integrity, privacy, and governance at scale, including schema drift handling, masking, and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Schema registry in Cloud Storage and Drift detection in Dataflow/Beam
- Row-level security with BigQuery policies for per-tenant isolation
- Per-tenant DLP masking and quarantine of invalid data
- Data Catalog lineage and end-to-end observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-Beam DoFn skeleton for schema validation
class ValidateSchema extends DoFn {
  processElement(context) {
    const record = context.element;
    // load canonical schema from GCS
    // validate fields, types, requireds
    if (valid(record)) {
      context.output(record);
    } else {
      context.output(NOT_VALID, record);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema drift in production without impacting traffic?
- How would you implement rollback across tenants if a schema change breaks downstream queries?
